  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="6b0727c9-51ee-5848-82d5-b900955d76de" activityNameId="f2acc3ea-3e9b-5c46-82da-03fd7a04a6ce" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Plugs, inlet and outlet, for network cable, at plant</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Plugs, inlet and outlet, for network cable, at plant
This dataset can be applied to describe the production of a typical set of plugs (inlet and outlet) for a computer network cable. The total weight of the plugs (2 identical ones) is 0.0086 kg. The information for this dataset is based on weighing, measurements and analysis at EMPA Laboratories. Updates were made in 2023 to reflect regionalized material and electricity mix for global production; UUID: 070ba622-2be7-3730-ac38-5e7c29c85c19
Describes the production of a typical set of inlet and outlet plugs of a computer network cable. Calculated per 1 unit of plugs, which is per definition 1 inlet and 1 outlet plug (identical in this case). Included are the materials PVC, HIPS and brass with their respective manufacturing processes. The infrastructure is calculated via the proxy "electronic component production plant". Further inventoried are the electricity for the assembly of the plugs, plus the road and rail transportation for input materials from the regional storage to the production site.
Location: The data describes a typical set of plugs for a computer network cable in 2006. Plugs and cable are produced by international manufacturers and available all over the world. Therefore a global dataset is justifiable.
Technology: The manufacturing process is represented by injection moulding and contouring (brass).
Time period: Unspecified
Production volume: unknown
Sampling: Weighing and measuring of materials. Literature for electricity consumption (assembly).
Extrapolations: see Geography and Technology
Uncertainty adjustments: see Geography and Technology
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_070ba622-2be7-3730-ac38-5e7c29c85c19.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
